{"item_id": "fresh_N362934", "reference_source": "ruling:N362934", "product": "You have submitted photographs and a detailed description of an item identified as a paper chain garland, item number 45534701, which consists of 48 strips of paper in eight bright colors, each measuring approximately 11.02” by 1.57”. The purchaser may assemble these strips into a paper chain garland measuring approximately 13’. Each strip features features pre-cut notches allowing the purchaser to simply interlock and clip the strips together without any additional tape, glue or fasteners. The item is flat packed in a transparent polybag with a printed header card. The flimsy paper decoration is marketed and sold as a single-use party decoration.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N362934, gold code 9505.90.4000:\nn of an item identified as a paper chain garland, item number 45534701, which consists of 48 strips of paper in eight bright colors, each measuring approximately 11.02” by 1.57”. The purchaser may assemble these strips into a paper chain garland measuring approximately 13’. Each strip features features pre-cut notches allowing the purchaser to simply interlock and clip the strips together without any additional tape, glue or fasteners. The item is flat packed in a transparent polybag with a printed header card. The flimsy paper decoration is marketed and sold as a single-use party decoration. The applicable subheading for the paper chain garland , item number 45534701, will be 9505.90.4000, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for “Festive, carnival or other entertainment articles, including magic tricks and practical joke articles; parts and accessories thereof: Other: Confetti, paper spirals or streamers, party favors and noisemakers; parts and accessories thereof.” The general rate of duty will be Free. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Sandra Carlson at sandra.carlson@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, (for)", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "9505.90.40.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 9505 festive, carnival or other entertainment articles; chapter 48 note 2 excludes articles of chapter 95, so paper is not classified in 4823", "GRI 2(a): unassembled strips with pre-cut notches have the essential character of the finished garland", "GRI 6: 9505.90 other; 9505.90.40 confetti, paper spirals or streamers, party favors and noisemakers (flimsy, disposable paper party decorations), statistical suffix .00"], "rationale": "The paper chain garland is a festive decoration of heading 9505, and chapter 48 excludes chapter 95 articles. CBP treats flimsy, disposable paper party decorations as party favors in 9505.90.40, as in NY N331026 (paper pop-up centerpiece, flimsy, one-time use), and in earlier rulings on paper garlands. The unassembled notched strips are classified as the finished garland under GRI 2(a).", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "9505.90.60.00", "reason": "'Other' festive articles; CBP places flimsy single-use paper party decorations in 9505.90.40"}, {"code": "4823.90.86", "reason": "articles of paper; chapter 48 excludes articles of chapter 95"}, {"code": "9505.10", "reason": "not a Christmas article"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N362939", "reference_source": "ruling:N362939", "product": "Famotidine Oral Suspension, imported in 40 mg/5 mL, is a histamine H2 receptor antagonist. It is indicated for the treatment of active duodenal ulcer (DU), active gastric ulcer (GU), symptomatic nonerosive gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), erosive esophagitis due to GERD, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N362939, gold code 3004.90.9254:\n 3004.90.9254 Ciara Horgan Northstar Healthcare UC 3300 Cork Airport Business Park Cork T12XN72 Ireland RE: The tariff classification of Famotidine Oral Suspension in dosage form, from India Dear Ms. Horgan: In your letter dated July 8, 2026, you requested a tariff classification ruling. Famotidine Oral Suspension, imported in 40 mg/5 mL, is a histamine H2 receptor antagonist. It is indicated for the treatment of active duodenal ulcer (DU), active gastric ulcer (GU), symptomatic nonerosive gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), erosive esophagitis due to GERD, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome. The applicable subheading for the Famotidine Oral Suspension in dosage form will be 3004.90.9254, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for “Medicaments … consisting of mixed or unmixed products for therapeutic or prophylactic uses, put up in measured doses … or in forms or packings for retail sale: Other: Other: Other: Medicaments primarily affecting the digestive system: Containing famotidine or its salts.” The general rate of duty will be free. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This merchandise may be subject to the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act and/or The Public Health Security and Bioterrorism Preparedness and Response Act of 2002 (The Bioterrorism Act), which are administered by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). Information on the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act, as well as The Bioterrorism Act, can be obtained by calling the FDA at 1-888-463-6332, or by visiting their website at www.fda.gov. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling ", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "3004.90.92.54", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 3004 covers medicaments for therapeutic use put up in measured doses or in forms or packings for retail sale; the oral suspension in dosage form qualifies", "GRI 6: 3004.90 other, 3004.90.92 other", "GRI 6: statistical suffix under Medicaments primarily affecting the digestive system: Containing famotidine or its salts (current 2026 Rev 20 tree, .54)"], "rationale": "Famotidine oral suspension in dosage form is a medicament of heading 3004, classified in 3004.90.92 as other medicaments. NY N339838 (in force) placed famotidine injection in dosage form in 3004.90.92 under the famotidine digestive-system line. In the current revision that statistical line is 3004.90.92.54, confirmed as a leaf by hts_navigate.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "2933.39", "reason": "bulk famotidine chemical; this product is in dosage form"}, {"code": "3004.90.92.56", "reason": "famotidine suffix cited in N339838 under the earlier tree; current revision places famotidine at .54"}, {"code": "3004.90.92.60", "reason": "residual digestive line; a specific famotidine line exists"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N362969", "reference_source": "ruling:N362969", "product": "Product information and photos were submitted for our review. The product under consideration is Item 865858, a decorative owl figure. The form of the figure is constructed of Styrofoam, and the form is covered with multiple materials. The eyes (the most prominent feature) of the owl are surrounded by natural, unspun sisal fibers. The belly of the owl is also covered with sisal fibers. The whole of the head (approximately 50 percent of the surface are of the figure) is covered with fine strands of cattails. The wings and tail feathers are made of a combination of birch wood twig slices, straw, and banana leaf. The owl is perched on a wooden twig with a pine cone and plastic pine needles, and it has plastic eyes. Both the sisal and cattail pieces qualify as plaiting materials. Together, they cover approximately 75 percent of the owl figure’s surface.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N362969, gold code 4602.19.8000:\nfibers, monofilament and strip and the like of plastics and strips of paper, but not strips of leather or composition leather or of felt or nonwovens, human hair, horsehair, textile rovings or yarns, or monofilament and strip and the like of chapter 54. Both the sisal and cattail pieces qualify as plaiting materials. Together, they cover approximately 75 percent of the owl figure’s surface. Such figures are classified by their covering material. Therefore, we will classify the figure as an article of plaiting material. As such, the figure is not classifiable in subheading 6307.90.9891, HTSUS. The applicable subheading for Item 865858, decorative owl figure, will be 4602.19.8000, HTSUS, which provides for Basketwork, wickerwork and other articles, made directly to shape from plaiting materials or made up from articles of heading 4601; articles of loofah: Of vegetable materials: Other: Other: Other: Other. The general rate of duty will be 2.3 percent ad valorem. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Laurel Duvall at laurel.duvall@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, (for) James P. Forkan Director National Commodity Specialist Division ", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "4602.19.80.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 3(b)", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: the figure is prima facie classifiable in several headings (4602 articles of plaiting materials, 3926 articles of plastics, 4421 wood) because it is a composite of different materials", "GRI 3(a): each heading covers only part of the materials, so equally specific", "GRI 3(b): the visible sisal and cattail plaiting materials covering about 75% of the surface give the owl its decorative appearance and essential character", "GRI 6: 4602.19 of vegetable materials, other; not willow, rattan, bamboo, wickerwork; 4602.19.80.00 other"], "rationale": "The owl is a composite good whose Styrofoam form is covered mostly (about 75 percent of surface) with sisal and cattail plaiting materials, which dominate its visual appearance and give it essential character under GRI 3(b). CBP classified a companion decorative hedgehog figure of the same construction in 4602.19.8000 (NY N362965, in force), and earlier sisal-covered animal figures likewise. It falls in the residual vegetable-material, non-wickerwork line 4602.19.80.00.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "3926.40.00.00", "reason": "Styrofoam form is hidden and does not give the essential character"}, {"code": "4421.99", "reason": "wood twig and slices are minor decorative accents"}, {"code": "9505.90.60.00", "reason": "owl figure has no festive association"}, {"code": "4602.19.60.00", "reason": "wickerwork line; the sisal and cattail covering is not wickerwork"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N363127", "reference_source": "ruling:N363127", "product": "Ltd. (El Monte, CA). The subject merchandise is Frozen Shrimp Spring Rolls. The product consists of a shrimp and vegetable filling enclosed in a rice flour wrapper. The filling contains shrimp (Penaeus vannamei, 44.45 percent), rice flour, water, bamboo shoots, water chestnuts, shiitake mushrooms, potato starch, chives, sugar, spring onions, sesame oil, ginger, salt, monosodium glutamate, and white pepper. Each spring roll weighs approximately 35 grams and is individually formed, quick-frozen, and packaged in airtight retail trays containing 20 pieces. The product is imported in a frozen state, requires cooking prior to consumption, and is intended for sale to the foodservice industry as an appetizer or snack.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N363127, gold code 1605.29.0500:\nle filling enclosed in a rice flour wrapper. The filling contains shrimp (Penaeus vannamei, 44.45 percent), rice flour, water, bamboo shoots, water chestnuts, shiitake mushrooms, potato starch, chives, sugar, spring onions, sesame oil, ginger, salt, monosodium glutamate, and white pepper. Each spring roll weighs approximately 35 grams and is individually formed, quick-frozen, and packaged in airtight retail trays containing 20 pieces. The product is imported in a frozen state, requires cooking prior to consumption, and is intended for sale to the foodservice industry as an appetizer or snack. The applicable subheading for the above-described Frozen Shrimp Spring Roll will be 1605.29.0500, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for: “Crustaceans, molluscs and other aquatic invertebrates, prepared or preserved: Shrimps and prawns: Other: Other: …Prepared meals.” The general rate of duty will be 5 percent ad valorem. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided on the World Wide Web at https://hts.usitc.gov/current. You have asked whether this product is subject to antidumping duties or countervailing duties (AD/CVD). When there is a question as to whether merchandise is subject to an antidumping or countervailing duty order or considered to be defined by the scope of an order, such a determination is governed by the U.S. Department of Commerce’s International Trade Administration (“Commerce”). Commerce issues scope rulings to determine whether merchandise which is in commercial production is covered by the scope of an antidumping or countervailing duty order. Commerce’s scope rulings are separate and distinct from decisions issued by CBP regarding tariff classification and country of origin for purposes such as duty assessment and marking. To seek a scope ruling, please visit the website of Commerce’s International Trade Administration. A guide to seeking a scope ruling can be found at https://access.trade.gov/help/Scope_Ruling_Guidance_(4.1.2022).pdf. A list of current AD/CVD investigations at the United States International Trade Commission can be viewed on its website at http://www.usitc.gov. Additionally, messages sent by Commerce to CBP regarding AD/CVD cash deposits and liquidation can be searched using ACE or CBP’s search tool at https://trade.cbp.dhs.gov/ace/adcvd/adcvd-public/#. Please note that seafood is subject to the Mandatory Country of Origin Labeling (COOL) requirements administered by the USDA’s Agricultural Marketing Service (AMS). We advise you to check with that agency for their further guidance on your scenario. Contact information for AMS is as follows: USDA-AMS-LS-SAT Room 2607-S, Stop 0254 1400 Independence Avenue, SW Washington, DC 20250-0254 Tel. 202.720.4486 Website: www.ams.usda.gov/COOL Email address for inquiries: COOL@usda.gov This merchandise is subject to The Public Health Security and Bioterrorism Preparedness and Response Act of 2002 (The Bioterrorism Act), which is regulated by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA). Information on the Bioterrorism Act can be obtained by calling FDA at 301-575-0156, or at the Web site www.fda.gov/oc/bioterrorism/bioact.html. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulati", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "1605.29.05.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: chapter 16 note 2: food preparations containing more than 20 percent by weight of crustaceans fall in chapter 16; shrimp at 44.45 percent exceeds 20 percent", "Chapter 16 note 2 exception for stuffed products of heading 1902 considered: the rice-flour spring roll wrapper is treated as a wrapper, not pasta (following HQ H341768 on shrimp dumplings with a non-pasta dough jacket)", "GRI 1: heading 1605 crustaceans, prepared or preserved", "GRI 6: 1605.2 shrimps and prawns; packaged in airtight trays, so 1605.29 (other than not in airtight containers)", "GRI 6: 1605.29.05.00 products containing fish meat; prepared meals"], "rationale": "The spring rolls contain 44.45 percent shrimp, so chapter 16 note 2 places the preparation in chapter 16 unless it is a stuffed pasta of 1902. Following HQ H341768, which placed frozen shrimp dumplings with a non-pasta starch wrapper in 1605.21.05 as a prepared shrimp product, the rolls are classified in heading 1605. Because the description states they are packed in airtight retail trays, the airtight-container subheading 1605.29 applies, at the 'prepared meals' line 1605.29.05.00. If the wrapper counts as pasta, the answer would be 1902.20.00.40.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "1902.20.00.40", "reason": "stuffed pasta, frozen; applies only if the rice-flour wrapper is pasta (as with the dim sum noodle rolls in N343875); a spring roll wrapper was treated as not pasta"}, {"code": "1605.21.05.00", "reason": "for goods not in airtight containers; the rolls are packed in airtight trays"}, {"code": "1905.90.10", "reason": "baked spring roll skins with vegetable filling (H305436); here shrimp exceeds 20 percent so chapter 16 note 2 applies"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N363254", "reference_source": "ruling:N363254", "product": "The item is called the Leafinator. It is a garden rake used in household yards. The item consists of a polypropylene (PP) upper rake panel, a polypropylene plastic lower scoop panel, an aluminum handle and aluminum pole, steel fasteners and steel hardware. The item measures approximately 60 inches long. The Leafinator allows a user to rake, gather, grip, lift, and transport yard debris while staying in a more upright position, helping to reduce repetitive bending and stooping and easing strain on the back, knees, and joints. The rake consists of polypropylene plastic upper and lower scoop panels, an aluminum handle and pole, steel fasteners and steel hardware. The polypropylene plastic components constitutes the majority of weight and bulk of the rake. Also, it performs the principal function of raking and picking up garden debris.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N363254, gold code 3924.90.5650:\nn on the back, knees, and joints. The item is considered a composite good within the meaning of General Rule of Interpretation (GRI) 3. The rake consists of polypropylene plastic upper and lower scoop panels, an aluminum handle and pole, steel fasteners and steel hardware. The polypropylene plastic components constitutes the majority of weight and bulk of the rake. Also, it performs the principal function of raking and picking up garden debris. Therefore, it is the opinion of this office that the polypropylene plastic components provides the essential character within the meaning of GRI 3(b). The applicable subheading of the Leafinator will be 3924.90.5650, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for “Tableware, kitchenware, other household articles and hygienic or toilet articles, of plastics: Other: Other. Other.” The general rate of duty will be 3.4 percent ad valorem. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Dana L. Salas at dana.l.salas@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, (for) James P. Forkan Director National Commodity Specialist Division ", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "3924.90.56.50", "deciding_gri": "GRI 3(b)", "gri_path": ["Chapter 82 note 1: hand tools of chapter 82 must have a blade, working edge or working surface of base metal; the working rake and scoop panels are plastic, so heading 8201 is excluded", "GRI 3(b): composite of plastic and aluminum; the polypropylene panels give essential character (majority of weight and bulk, perform the raking and scooping function)", "GRI 1: among plastic headings, 3924 other household articles of plastics covers a yard rake used around the household (as with plastic rake hands in N333477) over residual 3926", "GRI 6: 3924.90 other, 3924.90.56 other, suffix .50 other"], "rationale": "The rake cannot be a chapter 82 hand tool because its working surfaces are plastic, not base metal (chapter 82 note 1). As a composite of plastic panels and an aluminum pole, the polypropylene gives essential character under GRI 3(b), as stated in the facts. CBP classified plastic rake hands for scooping leaves and yard debris as household articles of plastics in 3924.90.5650 (NY N333477, in force), and this household yard rake follows that line.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8201.90.60.00", "reason": "chapter 82 note 1 requires a working part of base metal; the rake working parts are plastic"}, {"code": "7616.99", "reason": "aluminum handle/pole does not give essential character (plastic is majority of weight and bulk and performs the function)"}, {"code": "3926.90.99.89", "reason": "residual plastics heading; 3924 covers household articles and CBP treats household yard-debris rakes as such"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N363288", "reference_source": "ruling:N363288", "product": "A New Drug Application(NDA) has been submitted to the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) seeking approval for threeindications: non-erosive reflux disease (NERD) erosive esophagitis (EE)healing, and EE maintenanc, e.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N363288, gold code 3004.90.9260:\n Application(NDA) has been submitted to the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) seeking approval for threeindications: non-erosive reflux disease (NERD) erosive esophagitis (EE)healing, and EE maintenanc, e.In your letter, you suggest classification in subheading 3004.90.9255, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of theUnited States (HTSUS), which provides for “Containing pantoprazole or its salts.” We disagree. The subjectproduct is Tegoprazan and not Pantoprazole or its salts. This subheading is an eo nomine provision strictlyreserved for Pantoprazole or its salts and will be classified elsewhere.The applicable subheading for the Tegoprazan Tablets in dosage form will be 3004.90.9260, HTSUS, whichprovides for “Medicaments … consisting of mixed or unmixed products for therapeutic or prophylactic uses,put up in measured doses … or in forms or packings for retail sale: Other: Other: Other: Medicamentsprimarily affecting the digestive system: Other.” The general rate of duty will be free.The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are providedat https://hts.usitc.gov/.This merchandise may be subject to the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act and/or The Public HealthSecurity and Bioterrorism Preparedness and Response Act of 2002 (The Bioterrorism Act), which areadministered by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). Information on the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act, as well as The Bioterrorism Act, can be obtained by calling the FDA at 1-888-463-6332, or byvisiting their website at www.fda.gov.This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or othercharges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and otherduties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS.Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with eitherthe Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisionscovering exceptions to such tariffs.For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated CargoSystems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies.The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description asidentified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations(CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of theinformation furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate andcomplete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do notconform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs andBorder Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodicverification by CBP.This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of t", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "3004.90.92.60", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: if imported in measured doses or retail packings, heading 3004 medicaments; if in bulk, chapter 29 (heterocyclic compound) or 3003", "GRI 6 (assumed dosage form): 3004.90 other, 3004.90.92 other", "GRI 6: Medicaments primarily affecting the digestive system: no specific line for this active ingredient (pantoprazole, famotidine, ondansetron, glycopyrrolate, loperamide lines do not apply), so 'Other' .60"], "rationale": "The description gives only the NDA indications (NERD, erosive esophagitis healing and maintenance), not the product, its active ingredient or its form. Assuming a finished dosage-form medicament for acid-related digestive disease, it falls in heading 3004, subheading 3004.90.92, under the digestive-system 'Other' line 3004.90.92.60, as CBP did for lansoprazole tablets in NY N323546. If the active ingredient is imported in bulk, it would instead be a chapter 29 heterocyclic or sulfonamide compound.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "2934.99", "reason": "bulk active ingredient with N- and O-heterocycles (e.g., tegoprazan) would go here if imported in bulk; form not stated"}, {"code": "2935.90", "reason": "bulk vonoprazan (a sulfonamide) would go here if imported in bulk; form not stated"}, {"code": "3004.90.92.55", "reason": "pantoprazole line; no indication the drug is pantoprazole"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N363316", "reference_source": "ruling:N363316", "product": "The item at issue with this request is an aircraft cockpit Multifunction Control and Display Unit (MCDU) control panel subassembly, identified by part number 865-000163-2Z. The control panel subassembly is a component of the MCDU. A complete MCDU is installed in the cockpit of an aircraft and functions as the interface between the flight crew and the aircraft’s Flight Management System. The Flight Management System allows the the flight crew to input, select, modify, and display flight management information including the flight plan, navigation, and aircraft system data, et al. In it’s imported condition, the control panel subassembly cannot function as a complete MCDU because it does not contain an LCD display nor is it calibrated or tested for final use. The control panel subassembly is comprised of a number of components, including an electronic control assembly (ECA) which is a printed circuit board assembly populated with several push-button momentary contact switches, LEDs, resistors, photodiodes, a 40-pin electrical connector, and other minor electrical components. The ECA is further assembled onto a supportive backplate and front bezel with cutouts for the various push-button switches. After importation, an LCD display panel is added and the device is calibrated and tested.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N363316, gold code 8537.10.9170:\nwitches, LEDs, resistors, photodiodes, a 40-pin electrical connector, and other minor electrical components. The ECA is further assembled onto a supportive backplate and front bezel with cutouts for the various push-button switches. After importation, an LCD display panel is added and the device is calibrated and tested. In your request, you suggest classification in 8538.90.8180, Harmonized Tariff System of the United States (HTSUS) or alternatively, 8538.10.0000, HTSUS. We disagree on both accounts, as the item is neither a part of nor is it an unequipped base for the goods of heading 8537. The applicable subheading for the control panel subassembly will be 8537.10.9170, HTSUS, which provides for “Boards, panels, consoles, desks, cabinets and other bases, equipped with two or more apparatus of 8535 or 8536, for electric control or the distribution of electricity…: For a voltage not exceeding 1,000 V: Other: Other: Other.” The general rate of duty will be 2.7 percent ad valorem. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Gary Chaffee at gary.chaffee@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, (for) James P. Forkan Director National Commodity Specialist Division ", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8537.10.91.70", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 2(a) considered: without the LCD display the subassembly cannot input and display flight data, so it lacks the essential character of a complete MCDU and is classified as imported", "Section XVII note 2(f): electrical machinery and equipment of chapter 85 is not a 'part' of aircraft of chapter 88, so heading 8803 is excluded", "Section XVI note 2(a): a part that is itself a good of a chapter 85 heading is classified in that heading; the panel with two or more switches of heading 8536 mounted on a backplate and bezel is a panel equipped with apparatus of 8536 for electric control of heading 8537 (chapter 85 note 4 excludes populated boards from 8534)", "GRI 6: 8537.10 for a voltage not exceeding 1,000 V; 8537.10.91 other; suffix .70 other (not switchgear, numerical control, panel/distribution boards or programmable controllers)"], "rationale": "The control panel subassembly lacks the LCD display and calibration, so it does not have the essential character of a complete MCDU under GRI 2(a). Section XVII note 2(f) prevents it from being an aircraft part of 8803, and Section XVI note 2(a) classifies a part in its own heading when it is a named good. As a board and bezel equipped with multiple push-button switches of heading 8536 for electric control, it fits heading 8537, as CBP held for a switch-populated control PCB in NY N004021 (8537.10.9070), giving 8537.10.91.70. Classification as a part of the MCDU's own heading is a real alternative.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8803.90.90", "reason": "section XVII note 2(f) excludes chapter 85 electrical equipment from aircraft parts"}, {"code": "8471.60", "reason": "incomplete MCDU without display does not have the essential character of an input/output unit under GRI 2(a); also FMS-related equipment may fall outside 8471 under chapter 84 note 5(E)"}, {"code": "8538.90.81", "reason": "parts of 8537 apparatus; the subassembly is itself a panel equipped with two or more 8536 switches, a good of 8537 (section XVI note 2(a))"}, {"code": "9014.90", "reason": "parts of navigational instruments; chapter 90 note 2(a) keeps goods of a chapter 85 heading in that heading"}, {"code": "8534.00", "reason": "chapter 85 note on printed circuits excludes boards populated with other components"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N363384", "reference_source": "ruling:N363384", "product": "The outer shell is composed of cellular polyurethane plasticmaterial with a backing of 100% polyester woven fabric present merely for reinforcement purposes. Thejacket is lined with woven polyester fabric and quilted to a nonwoven polyester fill batting. The jacketfeatures a sherpa collar, a full front zipper closure, long sleeves with ribbed knit cuffs, one zippered chestpocket, two zippered pockets at the waist, and a ribbed knit bottom.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N363384, gold code 3926.20.9050:\nedule of the20.3000 UnitedStates (HTSUS). We disagree with your proposed classification. Chapter 59 Note 2(a)(5) states that\"Heading 5903 applies to: (a) Textile fabrics, impregnated, coated, covered or laminated with plastics… otherthan: (5) Plates, sheets or strip of cellular plastics, combined with textile fabric, where the textile fabric ispresent merely for reinforcing purposes (chapter 39).\" Because the textile fabric backing in this garment ispresent merely for reinforcing the cellular plastic, the jacket is classified as an apparel of plastic materialunder Chapter 39.Consequently, the applicable subheading for Style CB81426 will be 3926.20.9050, HTSUS, which providesfor “Other articles of plastics and articles of other materials of headings 3901 to 3914: Articles of apparel andclothing accessories (including gloves, mittens, and mitts): Other: Other: Other.” The general rate of dutywill be 5 percent ad valorem.The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change.The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or othercharges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and otherduties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS.Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with eitherthe Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisionscovering exceptions to such tariffs.For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated CargoSystems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies.The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description asidentified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations(CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of theinformation furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate andcomplete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do notconform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs andBorder Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2.Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodicverification by CBP.This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border ProtectionRegulations (19 C.F.R. 177).A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documentsfiled at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contactNational Import Specialist Rosemarie Hayward at rosemariecasey.hayward@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, (for)James P. ForkanDirectorNational Commodity Specialist Division ", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "3926.20.90.50", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["Chapter 59 note 2(a)(5): textile fabrics combined with cellular plastics where the textile is present merely for reinforcement are excluded from heading 5903 and are treated as plastics of chapter 39", "Chapter 62 note/section XI: the outer shell is therefore not a textile fabric, so the garment is not apparel of chapters 61/62 (which require textile outer shell)", "GRI 1 / GRI 3(b): outer shell of plastic gives the jacket its essential character; heading 3926 articles of apparel of plastics", "GRI 6: 3926.20 articles of apparel; not seamless, not PVC rainwear valued not over $10; 3926.20.90 other; suffix .50 other (not aprons)"], "rationale": "The outer shell is cellular polyurethane with a woven polyester backing present merely for reinforcement, so under chapter 59 note 2(a)(5) the shell material is a plastic of chapter 39, not a textile. The jacket is therefore an article of apparel of plastics of heading 3926, and CBP has classified such faux suede/cellular PU jackets in 3926.20.9050 (NY N326796, in force). The gender of the wearer does not affect this line.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "6201.40 / 6202.40", "reason": "outer shell is cellular plastic with backing merely for reinforcement, which is a chapter 39 material under chapter 59 note 2(a)(5), not a textile of chapter 62"}, {"code": "6210.40 / 6210.50", "reason": "garments of 5903 fabrics require a textile fabric coated/laminated with plastics; here the textile is merely reinforcement for cellular plastic, excluded from 5903"}, {"code": "3926.20.60.00", "reason": "PVC rainwear valued not over $10; this shell is polyurethane"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N363422", "reference_source": "ruling:N363422", "product": "Product information and photos were submitted for our review. The product under consideration is an energy storage cabinet. You describe the cabinet as being imported in unassembled form, but complete, and then assembled in the United States. You describe the cabinet as \"Complete Knocked Down” and designed for use in a “Battery Energy Storage System”. The cabinet measures 6058mm by 2438mm by 2896mm, or 42,722 liters in volume. It is constructed of steel. The steel cabinet does not contain any electrical or mechanical components at time of importation. The cabinet is designed to hold batteries and electrical equipment to function as a power source. The unassembled cabinet includes wall modules, a floor module, a roof module, and battery racks. You state that the energy storage cabinet structure divides the interior into dedicated compartments for battery modules, electrical equipment, control systems, and thermal management equipment.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N363422, gold code 7309.00.0090:\nhat article incomplete or unfinished, provided that, as entered, the incomplete or unfinished article has the essential character of the complete or finished article. It shall also include a reference to that article complete or finished (or falling to be classified as complete or finished by virtue of this rule), entered unassembled or disassembled. The energy storage cabinet kit is the complete cabinet, but unassembled. In accordance with GRI 2(a), the unassembled storage cabinet will be classified in the same manner as a fully assembled storage cabinet. The cabinet is a container of steel. The applicable subheading for the energy storage cabinet will be 7309.00.0090, HTSUS, which provides for Reservoirs, tanks, vats and similar containers for any material (other than compressed or liquefied gas), of iron or steel, of a capacity exceeding 300 liters, whether or not lined or heat insulated, but not fitted with mechanical or thermal equipment: Other. The general rate of duty will be free. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Claudia Lin at chingju.lin@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, (for) James P. Forkan Director National Commodity Specialist Division ", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "7309.00.00.90", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 2(a): the unassembled but complete cabinet (wall, floor, roof modules and racks) is classified as the assembled article", "GRI 1: heading 7309 covers reservoirs, tanks, vats and similar containers for any material, of iron or steel, capacity over 300 liters, not fitted with mechanical or thermal equipment; the empty steel BESS cabinet of 42,722 liters fits (HQ H329722 on BESS containers)", "Section XVI note 2 not reached: the empty cabinet is not itself a part of an accumulator solely/principally used with one battery, and heading 7309 describes it; not 8537 because no electrical apparatus is fitted (contrast HQ H356254)", "GRI 6: 7309.00.00 suffix .90 other (not a tank)"], "rationale": "The steel BESS cabinet, imported complete but unassembled, is classified as the assembled article under GRI 2(a). HQ H329722 (in force) held empty steel BESS containers designed to hold batteries, without mechanical or thermal equipment, to be containers of heading 7309 rather than freight containers of 8609. This cabinet has a capacity of 42,722 liters and no fitted equipment, so it falls in 7309.00.00, statistical line .90 (other than tanks).", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8609.00.00", "reason": "not specially designed and equipped for carriage of goods by one or more modes of transport; it is a stationary equipment enclosure (H329722 rejected 8609 for BESS containers)"}, {"code": "8507.90.80.00", "reason": "parts of accumulators (N359756 small enclosure weldment of a battery pack); this large empty steel container is described by 7309 as a container for any material"}, {"code": "8537.20.00", "reason": "requires boards/cabinets equipped with two or more apparatus of 8535/8536; no electrical components at importation"}, {"code": "9403.20.00", "reason": "not furniture; a large industrial container over 300 liters"}, {"code": "7308.90", "reason": "structures; the cabinet is a closed container for housing goods, more specifically described by 7309"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N363423", "reference_source": "ruling:N363423", "product": "The products under consideration are polyethylene foam packaging inserts used to protect a robotic arm during shipment. The custom-configured foam is cut to profile and hollowed with product-specific internal cavities.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N363423, gold code 3923.90.0080:\nrd Bots 35 Garvies Point Rd. Glen Cove, NY 11542 RE: The tariff classification of polyethylene foam packaging inserts from China Dear Mr. Heath: In your letter dated July 18, 2026, you requested a tariff classification ruling. The products under consideration are polyethylene foam packaging inserts used to protect a robotic arm during shipment. The custom-configured foam is cut to profile and hollowed with product-specific internal cavities. In your request, you proposed classification under subheading 3923.90.0080, HTSUS, as a plastic article for the conveyance or packing of goods. We agree. The applicable subheading for the polyethylene foam packaging inserts will be 3923.90.0080, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for articles for the conveyance or packing of goods, of plastics; stoppers, lids, caps and other closures, of plastics: other: other. The general rate of duty will be 3 percent ad valorem. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist ChingJu (Claudia) Lin at chingju.lin@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, (for) James P. Forkan Director National Commodity Specialist Division ", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "3923.90.00.80", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: articles for the conveyance or packing of goods, of plastics, heading 3923; the foam is cut and hollowed into a dedicated packing article, so it is no longer a plate/sheet of 3921", "GRI 5(b) not needed: the inserts are imported as packing articles in their own right", "GRI 6: 3923.90.00 other (not boxes, bags, carboys, spools, closures); suffix .80 other (not pails)"], "rationale": "The polyethylene foam inserts are cut to profile and hollowed with cavities to hold and protect a robotic arm in shipment, which makes them articles for the conveyance or packing of goods of heading 3923 rather than cellular plastic sheet of 3921. CBP classified a plastic foam packing insert in 3923.90.0080 (NY H89540, in force). The residual statistical line .80 applies since they are not pails.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "3921.11.00.00", "reason": "cellular plastic plates/sheets; the foam is cut to profile with product-specific cavities, making it a finished packing article"}, {"code": "3923.10.00", "reason": "boxes, cases and crates; an insert is not a box"}, {"code": "3926.90.99.89", "reason": "residual plastics heading; 3923 specifically covers articles for packing goods"}]}}
